**Fix a char-boundary panic in `filter_noisy_patterns` (`src/format/output.rs`).**

• **The bug**: the git-log filter checked `hash_part[..40]` (and later sliced `[..7]`) *before* verifying the bytes were ASCII hex. A tool-output line like `commit ✓✓✓…` — ≥47 bytes where byte 40 falls inside a multi-byte UTF-8 char — panicked with `end byte index 40 is not a char boundary` (reproduced standalone before fixing).
• **The fix**: check the first 40 bytes with `as_bytes().iter().take(40).all(|b| b.is_ascii_hexdigit())` instead of slicing. Since a real git hash is all-ASCII, `[..7]` is guaranteed to land on a char boundary once the byte check passes. Non-hash unicode lines now pass through untouched.
• **The test**: `test_noisy_commit_line_unicode_no_panic` — a regression test that panics before the fix and asserts pass-through after.

Verified: `cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo build`, `cargo test` (4294 lib + 88 integration passing) all green. Committed as `cf75723`.
